WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:14.200
Hello everyone, and welcome to the CONNEXUS Forum.

2
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:18.990
I'm Alexander Minchev, co-founder and CEO of AbilikSoft.

3
00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:24.920
I have been working on physical security since my military duty,

4
00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:31.760
when I had to protect the military prison and take care

5
00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.190
for prisoners not to escape.

6
00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:40.830
And in the last 30 years, I've been

7
00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:46.360
working on servers. I started working when the servers were

8
00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:51.360
really connected to the internet, and I continue to this day.

9
00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.360
Currently, AbilikSoft has a presence in three data centers

10
00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.880
across the country, and we have our own carrier-neutral data center in

11
00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:06.440
London, which gives me a lot of vision about

12
00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:12.280
what can be done, and in my career I have seen things done, let's

13
00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:16.510
say, properly, and I have seen them done in a very questionable way.

14
00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.710
So first of all, we need to think about responsibility.

15
00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:25.800
In this whole presentation, we will speak about the physical

16
00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:31.560
layer, the bottom of the ship, and it's not so interesting as the

17
00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:36.750
top, but problems at the bottom will sink the ship really quick.

18
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.230
So, we need to know who is responsible.

19
00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:44.960
If you have your data in a cloud, and by cloud I mean any type of

20
00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:50.040
cloud service, software as a service, if you have your data in

21
00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:55.440
your partner's or your vendor's premises, you've delegated the risk and

22
00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:59.932
the risks, we'll talk about them later, the risks are taken care of by a

23
00:01:59.932 --> 00:02:03.052
vendor. You should be careful in choosing a vendor,

24
00:02:03.052 --> 00:02:06.042
but yet you have delegated these risks.

25
00:02:06.772 --> 00:02:11.172
If you have your data on your premises, or part of your data,

26
00:02:11.172 --> 00:02:15.372
of course, you keep the risk and you are fully responsible for what

27
00:02:15.372 --> 00:02:18.732
happens to your data and how this affects your business and your

28
00:02:18.732 --> 00:02:25.972
customers. And if you choose to use an external data center,

29
00:02:25.972 --> 00:02:30.972
you share the risk. I mean, the data center obviously takes care of

30
00:02:30.972 --> 00:02:35.172
power, physical security, fire extinguishing system, and much,

31
00:02:35.172 --> 00:02:39.132
much more. But yet you are responsible for your own servers,

32
00:02:39.132 --> 00:02:42.252
backup strategy, infrastructure, and of course for the list of

33
00:02:42.252 --> 00:02:44.442
people who can enter the facility.

34
00:02:45.052 --> 00:02:49.252
And speaking about risks, at the end there are only three types of

35
00:02:49.252 --> 00:02:52.252
risks that you should take care of.

36
00:02:52.252 --> 00:02:55.492
The first risk is data leakage.

37
00:02:55.492 --> 00:03:01.492
In this scenario, someone gets a copy of your data, and this can be a

38
00:03:01.492 --> 00:03:04.052
one-time event or it can be continuous.

39
00:03:04.052 --> 00:03:08.492
In the worst cases, the data can be continuously accessed for,

40
00:03:08.492 --> 00:03:09.442
let's say, years.

41
00:03:10.532 --> 00:03:12.242
The second risk is

42
00:03:13.572 --> 00:03:17.482
someone or something destroying or modifying your data.

43
00:03:17.892 --> 00:03:22.882
And in this case, you can lose all of your data and your business

44
00:03:23.452 --> 00:03:25.242
actually by this accident.

45
00:03:25.892 --> 00:03:32.772
And, sorry, the third risk

46
00:03:32.772 --> 00:03:36.692
is to be cut off from your data.

47
00:03:36.692 --> 00:03:40.052
In this scenario, no data is modified or deleted,

48
00:03:40.052 --> 00:03:44.852
but for a certain period of time, you don't have access to your data

49
00:03:44.852 --> 00:03:47.082
and you probably cannot operate.

50
00:03:47.612 --> 00:03:53.852
All we speak about today is these three risks, and in the period

51
00:03:53.852 --> 00:03:58.442
of new and new legal requirements, we as business owners should

52
00:03:59.172 --> 00:04:01.402
take care to be sure that we are

53
00:04:02.494 --> 00:04:07.294
covering all these risks and we are prepared for them.

54
00:04:07.294 --> 00:04:11.334
And speaking about risks, let's start outside.

55
00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:15.564
Ivaylo. Thank you, Alexander, and welcome everyone.

56
00:04:16.014 --> 00:04:20.014
I'm Ivaylo Konev, a pre-sales engineer with more than 20 years of

57
00:04:20.014 --> 00:04:23.054
experience working with server, storage, and networking

58
00:04:23.054 --> 00:04:27.414
infrastructure. Over the years, I've seen technology evolve from

59
00:04:27.414 --> 00:04:32.574
traditional physical infrastructure and enterprise infrastructure to

60
00:04:32.574 --> 00:04:36.884
today's highly connected and increasingly demanding

61
00:04:38.294 --> 00:04:39.964
data center operations.

62
00:04:40.694 --> 00:04:44.294
So, today I'm working for a data center operator with four data

63
00:04:44.294 --> 00:04:48.844
centers in Bulgaria, and I have the perspective

64
00:04:49.974 --> 00:04:51.764
to see the technology

65
00:04:52.734 --> 00:04:55.614
and the challenges not only from the technology side,

66
00:04:55.614 --> 00:04:59.774
but also from the side of the company operating this critical

67
00:04:59.774 --> 00:05:05.134
infrastructure. And this is what I want to share with you

68
00:05:05.134 --> 00:05:09.894
today. What happens when something that we normally take for

69
00:05:09.894 --> 00:05:13.894
granted, power, connectivity, or physical infrastructure,

70
00:05:13.894 --> 00:05:18.894
suddenly disappears? So, let me start with a very simple scenario or

71
00:05:18.894 --> 00:05:21.974
question. What happens if someone cuts the cable?

72
00:05:21.974 --> 00:05:24.414
I don't mean the cable connecting your office.

73
00:05:24.414 --> 00:05:31.254
I mean the cable connecting countries,

74
00:05:31.254 --> 00:05:35.174
sometimes thousands or hundreds of kilometers long,

75
00:05:35.174 --> 00:05:36.884
and much of it lies on the seabed.

76
00:05:37.614 --> 00:05:44.174
And so, we like to think of the internet as something

77
00:05:44.174 --> 00:05:46.684
that exists in the cloud, but the cloud is physical.

78
00:05:47.134 --> 00:05:52.694
Every cloud service, video call, video conference call,

79
00:05:52.694 --> 00:05:59.214
AI workload, financial transaction, they have physical

80
00:05:59.214 --> 00:06:03.974
infrastructure, and a significant part of it is under the sea.

81
00:06:03.974 --> 00:06:07.085
And when that infrastructure fails,

82
00:06:08.335 --> 00:06:11.895
the consequences can be much bigger than the

83
00:06:11.895 --> 00:06:13.885
point where the damage occurs.

84
00:06:15.135 --> 00:06:18.685
So, let me give you an example, the Baltic lesson,

85
00:06:19.415 --> 00:06:23.645
while also reminding you what happened during 2024 and 2025.

86
00:06:24.175 --> 00:06:28.015
We saw several incidents involving submarine cables and other

87
00:06:28.015 --> 00:06:31.245
critical infrastructure in the Baltic Sea.

88
00:06:31.855 --> 00:06:36.255
Some were investigated as accidents, while others raised concern

89
00:06:36.255 --> 00:06:38.495
about deliberate interference.

90
00:06:38.495 --> 00:06:41.725
But no matter the cause, there is one important lesson:

91
00:06:42.135 --> 00:06:45.725
our digital infrastructure has physical failure points.

92
00:06:46.335 --> 00:06:50.805
So, an anchor, fishing activity,

93
00:06:51.855 --> 00:06:56.175
an accident, or deliberate damage can interrupt a critical

94
00:06:56.175 --> 00:07:02.015
connection. And replacing a submarine cable is not just like

95
00:07:02.015 --> 00:07:03.935
changing a network switch.

96
00:07:03.935 --> 00:07:06.965
You have to locate the fault, mobilize a vessel,

97
00:07:07.695 --> 00:07:10.015
go to the site, repair the cable.

98
00:07:10.015 --> 00:07:13.765
And that takes time and, you probably know, a lot of money.

99
00:07:14.695 --> 00:07:16.565
What does this mean for the Black Sea?

100
00:07:17.335 --> 00:07:22.175
The Black Sea is an important connectivity route

101
00:07:22.175 --> 00:07:24.805
between Europe, Turkey, and the Caucasus.

102
00:07:25.535 --> 00:07:29.735
But what happens if that route goes down?

103
00:07:29.735 --> 00:07:35.975
You can have terabits of bandwidth, but if all

104
00:07:35.975 --> 00:07:38.975
this bandwidth uses the same physical corridor,

105
00:07:38.975 --> 00:07:40.335
this is not resilience.

106
00:07:40.335 --> 00:07:43.575
So, we should ask three important questions here.

107
00:07:43.575 --> 00:07:45.415
Where does the cable go?

108
00:07:45.415 --> 00:07:47.015
What happens if it fails?

109
00:07:47.015 --> 00:07:49.975
And how quickly can we repair it?

110
00:07:49.975 --> 00:07:53.735
Because a second fiber doesn't mean real redundancy.

111
00:07:53.735 --> 00:07:57.565
Real redundancy means having another physical path.

112
00:07:58.015 --> 00:08:02.855
We can have redundant power, cooling, or another redundant network,

113
00:08:02.855 --> 00:08:07.777
but the weakest link still remains thousands of kilometers under the

114
00:08:07.777 --> 00:08:11.897
seabed. So, how much bandwidth we have is not a

115
00:08:11.897 --> 00:08:16.297
question. The question, more importantly, is: if this route goes down,

116
00:08:16.297 --> 00:08:19.697
what happens next? And how would you get there?

117
00:08:19.697 --> 00:08:22.457
Because sometimes someone cuts the cable.

118
00:08:22.457 --> 00:08:24.527
And with that, Alexander, back to you.

119
00:08:25.897 --> 00:08:29.977
Thank you, Ivo. So, the next thing we should discuss is what

120
00:08:29.977 --> 00:08:32.417
happens if someone walks in.

121
00:08:32.417 --> 00:08:36.417
There are very different scenarios depending on where our data

122
00:08:36.417 --> 00:08:40.657
is. Obviously, if you trusted someone to keep your data,

123
00:08:40.657 --> 00:08:43.367
and we are speaking about cloud and software as a service,

124
00:08:43.777 --> 00:08:48.857
you have them cover the risk of someone walking in.

125
00:08:48.857 --> 00:08:51.657
They have to do this, but it's not your responsibility,

126
00:08:51.657 --> 00:08:53.617
and you cannot control this.

127
00:08:53.617 --> 00:08:57.287
And in case your data is in someone else's premises,

128
00:08:57.777 --> 00:09:00.497
the thing that matters the most is what you share,

129
00:09:00.497 --> 00:09:04.887
of course, and the physical security of your own devices.

130
00:09:05.417 --> 00:09:09.897
It can take only 30 seconds for someone with access to an

131
00:09:09.897 --> 00:09:14.057
unlocked laptop to do a lot of damage.

132
00:09:14.057 --> 00:09:16.687
The cloud providers themselves work continuously.

133
00:09:17.257 --> 00:09:20.977
We have strong passwords, we have two-factor authentication,

134
00:09:20.977 --> 00:09:25.697
we have passphrases, but yet, for a certain period

135
00:09:25.697 --> 00:09:29.097
of time, your laptop or your mobile is you.

136
00:09:29.097 --> 00:09:35.657
The next scenario is if you trusted a data center to

137
00:09:35.657 --> 00:09:37.447
keep your own servers inside.

138
00:09:37.857 --> 00:09:41.807
In data centers, access is inconvenient by design.

139
00:09:42.257 --> 00:09:45.167
We trade convenience for security.

140
00:09:45.657 --> 00:09:49.017
So, you have to request a visit in advance, you have your identity

141
00:09:49.017 --> 00:09:53.457
checked, your picture is compared with a previously stored

142
00:09:53.457 --> 00:09:57.257
one, you're guided, there are mantraps, cameras,

143
00:09:57.257 --> 00:10:02.807
and so on. So, basically, in this scenario, physical security is

144
00:10:03.337 --> 00:10:05.377
usually on a high level.

145
00:10:05.377 --> 00:10:09.617
And your main responsibility is the list of people who can enter the

146
00:10:09.617 --> 00:10:14.698
facility. If a colleague who left the company 2 years ago is still

147
00:10:14.698 --> 00:10:16.768
on that list, you may have a problem.

148
00:10:17.298 --> 00:10:21.738
And usually the biggest problem for the physical security in the

149
00:10:21.738 --> 00:10:24.978
scenario of someone walking in is the office.

150
00:10:24.978 --> 00:10:26.698
I mean, offices are very different.

151
00:10:26.698 --> 00:10:30.618
Some are like banks, but most of them are not that

152
00:10:30.618 --> 00:10:35.898
secure. And even if the office has a dedicated, let's say, server

153
00:10:35.898 --> 00:10:41.538
room, usually there are still fewer measures and fewer checks, and

154
00:10:41.538 --> 00:10:43.368
people can actually

155
00:10:44.338 --> 00:10:46.498
enter relatively easily.

156
00:10:46.498 --> 00:10:51.728
So, usually the locks work, but the procedures fail.

157
00:10:52.218 --> 00:10:58.018
And this is mostly about the physical security.

158
00:10:58.018 --> 00:11:01.178
Now, Ivaylo, you tell us about other risks.

159
00:11:01.178 --> 00:11:03.288
Thank you, Alexander. So,

160
00:11:04.338 --> 00:11:07.138
let me start with a very simple scenario now.

161
00:11:07.138 --> 00:11:08.968
So, someone paused the clock.

162
00:11:09.698 --> 00:11:11.088
And this is the next slide.

163
00:11:11.658 --> 00:11:13.568
Okay, maybe the power is down.

164
00:11:14.098 --> 00:11:16.138
Maybe the cooling system stops.

165
00:11:16.138 --> 00:11:17.538
Suddenly we have a problem.

166
00:11:17.538 --> 00:11:20.578
What happens next? Let's follow it minute by minute.

167
00:11:20.578 --> 00:11:23.858
But I see the clock in front of me, so I'll try to shorten the

168
00:11:23.858 --> 00:11:26.448
presentation a little bit in this part of my speech.

169
00:11:26.978 --> 00:11:29.128
So, minute zero, probably

170
00:11:30.098 --> 00:11:34.618
nothing has happened. The power disappears, but everything is

171
00:11:34.618 --> 00:11:38.098
properly designed. The servers do not immediately go down.

172
00:11:38.098 --> 00:11:43.058
The UPS should take over, and this is what the UPS are supposed to

173
00:11:43.058 --> 00:11:47.818
do. But it is important to know the UPS is not the solution.

174
00:11:47.818 --> 00:11:49.778
They are the bridge to the solution.

175
00:11:49.778 --> 00:11:53.858
For the first minutes, the UPS is carrying the IT load, but

176
00:11:53.858 --> 00:11:55.688
the generators should start.

177
00:11:56.138 --> 00:12:02.418
And now we have the time to start asking the question what

178
00:12:02.418 --> 00:12:07.458
happens. From the 5th to the 15th minute, the generators are online,

179
00:12:07.458 --> 00:12:11.098
but if you are the customer, you probably do not notice

180
00:12:11.098 --> 00:12:13.899
anything because your server, your application,

181
00:12:13.899 --> 00:12:15.609
your network is still operating.

182
00:12:16.059 --> 00:12:20.179
This is the point where the facility design matters.

183
00:12:20.179 --> 00:12:26.459
If the design is done badly, maybe everything can start

184
00:12:26.459 --> 00:12:30.579
falling apart. The UPS can carry the IT load,

185
00:12:30.579 --> 00:12:33.929
the cooling stops, and at some point everything finishes.

186
00:12:34.499 --> 00:12:39.059
But if the facility is designed well, this is different.

187
00:12:39.059 --> 00:12:42.899
The backup system is working, the cooling system is still working,

188
00:12:42.899 --> 00:12:44.619
the generators are stable.

189
00:12:44.619 --> 00:12:47.579
And most importantly, we have time to react.

190
00:12:47.579 --> 00:12:50.739
We have time to find the problem and to fix it.

191
00:12:50.739 --> 00:12:56.059
We have time to keep the facility running until the primary power

192
00:12:56.059 --> 00:13:00.659
is restored. And now the question is not, did the UPS work?

193
00:13:00.659 --> 00:13:05.739
The question is, did the whole facility design give us enough time

194
00:13:05.739 --> 00:13:08.859
to react? And then comes the dangerous part.

195
00:13:08.859 --> 00:13:12.779
Imagine that the generators and the UPS are okay,

196
00:13:12.779 --> 00:13:15.979
but the cooling stops. The servers can continue working,

197
00:13:15.979 --> 00:13:21.299
but the heat continues and goes up very fast.

198
00:13:21.299 --> 00:13:27.659
So, with today's AI GPU workloads, this heat builds up very

199
00:13:27.659 --> 00:13:31.739
fast. So, the question changes once again.

200
00:13:31.739 --> 00:13:35.579
It's no longer how long the UPS batteries will last,

201
00:13:35.579 --> 00:13:38.569
it is how long until the heat becomes the problem.

202
00:13:39.459 --> 00:13:45.299
And now imagine you're the person who is responsible for the

203
00:13:45.299 --> 00:13:48.699
facility. The power is gone, but the UPS has taken over,

204
00:13:48.699 --> 00:13:52.219
so you have time to sit down and relax and to think about what

205
00:13:52.219 --> 00:13:55.899
happens next and to find the problem and to fix it.

206
00:13:55.899 --> 00:13:59.299
And that is what real resilience is about.

207
00:13:59.299 --> 00:14:03.569
It's not avoiding every failure, but having enough time to react.

208
00:14:04.259 --> 00:14:08.259
Because sooner or later someone pulls the plug.

209
00:14:08.259 --> 00:14:11.339
Alex, back to you. So, thank you.

210
00:14:11.339 --> 00:14:14.739
Well, the next question is quite important.

211
00:14:14.739 --> 00:14:16.371
Where is your data actually?

212
00:14:16.861 --> 00:14:20.211
Not in which application, not in which service,

213
00:14:20.661 --> 00:14:25.301
but at the end of the day, your information is somewhere

214
00:14:25.301 --> 00:14:31.421
on some servers, on some disk, in some kind of redundancy

215
00:14:31.421 --> 00:14:32.331
infrastructure usually.

216
00:14:33.381 --> 00:14:40.301
So even if we have our whole ship, okay,

217
00:14:40.301 --> 00:14:45.701
if the cables survived, if nobody walks in and so on and so on,

218
00:14:45.701 --> 00:14:48.731
we still have to think about your data.

219
00:14:50.101 --> 00:14:53.571
So data usually spreads.

220
00:14:54.341 --> 00:15:00.661
Data usually walks to more places than we originally

221
00:15:00.661 --> 00:15:04.661
intend to. It's like a server and its backup,

222
00:15:04.661 --> 00:15:08.421
all of the computers that have a cached copy of the data,

223
00:15:08.421 --> 00:15:12.781
these phones that sync all the time, this database dump that

224
00:15:12.781 --> 00:15:15.781
someone took home to finish his or her work.

225
00:15:15.781 --> 00:15:20.061
The data actually tends to spread, which is important for the

226
00:15:20.061 --> 00:15:23.941
risk of giving someone a copy of your data.

227
00:15:23.941 --> 00:15:29.381
And this is the first risk: to have too many copies of your data

228
00:15:29.381 --> 00:15:34.181
in too many places. But the second risk is to have too few

229
00:15:34.181 --> 00:15:37.021
copies. Let's say that we have a server, a single server,

230
00:15:37.021 --> 00:15:40.901
let's say in your own facility, in your office.

231
00:15:40.901 --> 00:15:44.501
And this server has a single backup device next to it.

232
00:15:44.501 --> 00:15:47.021
It's more common than you can imagine.

233
00:15:47.021 --> 00:15:50.581
And then something can kill the server and the backup at the same

234
00:15:50.581 --> 00:15:54.981
time. And you will probably lose all of your

235
00:15:54.981 --> 00:16:00.221
data forever. So the question is not to have as many copies as

236
00:16:00.221 --> 00:16:04.251
possible, and definitely not to have as few copies as possible,

237
00:16:04.941 --> 00:16:09.941
but rather to have the required number of copies

238
00:16:09.941 --> 00:16:15.861
for the goals your business has, and to know why and where

239
00:16:15.861 --> 00:16:21.422
these copies are. But it's important to think about this

240
00:16:21.422 --> 00:16:27.182
copy in one more way that many companies do not think

241
00:16:27.182 --> 00:16:30.692
about. It's: where are these copies?

242
00:16:32.022 --> 00:16:34.652
Okay. And the last part, I promise.

243
00:16:35.662 --> 00:16:38.982
So, now imagine we have a facility with redundant power,

244
00:16:38.982 --> 00:16:42.212
redundant cooling, and redundant network connectivity.

245
00:16:42.782 --> 00:16:46.942
Is it resilient? Maybe. But let me change one thing.

246
00:16:46.942 --> 00:16:50.932
What if all that redundancy is inside the same building?

247
00:16:51.902 --> 00:16:55.502
Alex? If we have all this data in the same building,

248
00:16:55.502 --> 00:17:00.142
it's relatively easy to have at least two of the risks

249
00:17:00.142 --> 00:17:04.422
materialize. First of all, something can happen

250
00:17:04.422 --> 00:17:07.142
to the whole building, like fire, let's say.

251
00:17:07.142 --> 00:17:11.942
And second of all, the connectivity to this building can be

252
00:17:11.942 --> 00:17:17.822
cut if this connectivity goes through one path only.

253
00:17:17.822 --> 00:17:24.422
So it's important to realize that having a single building for

254
00:17:24.422 --> 00:17:27.782
your servers, or having a single availability zone for your

255
00:17:27.782 --> 00:17:31.022
cloud services, is not enough for a lot of scenarios.

256
00:17:31.022 --> 00:17:33.062
It depends on what you are risking, of course,

257
00:17:33.062 --> 00:17:34.702
and what your goals are.

258
00:17:34.702 --> 00:17:40.222
And you often need to think about this in more than one

259
00:17:40.222 --> 00:17:43.812
way. It's about geographic resilience.

260
00:17:44.342 --> 00:17:48.982
Exactly. And this is something that we sometimes forget when

261
00:17:48.982 --> 00:17:50.452
designing critical infrastructure.

262
00:17:50.942 --> 00:17:52.942
We think in terms of components.

263
00:17:52.942 --> 00:17:55.382
Two UPS systems, two cooling systems,

264
00:17:55.382 --> 00:17:57.822
two power paths, etc.

265
00:17:57.822 --> 00:18:01.182
But resilience is not only having two of something, two of

266
00:18:01.182 --> 00:18:06.062
something. Resilience is making sure that the same event cannot

267
00:18:06.062 --> 00:18:09.582
take them both out. Yes.

268
00:18:09.582 --> 00:18:14.622
Ideally, you have to have at least two places for your

269
00:18:14.622 --> 00:18:16.492
important data and applications.

270
00:18:17.102 --> 00:18:21.303
And ideally, these places should be geographically distributed in

271
00:18:21.303 --> 00:18:24.933
different earthquake zones, different electricity providers,

272
00:18:25.743 --> 00:18:27.663
different physical access rules.

273
00:18:27.663 --> 00:18:30.623
This is the true key to continuous operation for important

274
00:18:30.623 --> 00:18:33.863
businesses. Or let's take power.

275
00:18:33.863 --> 00:18:35.693
If independent

276
00:18:36.743 --> 00:18:40.533
electrical feeds can look like excellent redundancy,

277
00:18:41.023 --> 00:18:43.653
but if both of them ultimately

278
00:18:45.703 --> 00:18:49.693
end up in the same substation, same physical corridor or same building,

279
00:18:50.223 --> 00:18:54.343
maybe it is only logical redundancy,

280
00:18:54.343 --> 00:18:56.333
but not geographical redundancy.

281
00:18:58.503 --> 00:19:01.223
Thank you, everyone, for your time.

282
00:19:01.223 --> 00:19:02.893
We can answer questions later.

283
00:19:03.383 --> 00:19:04.283
Thank you.
